=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The NOC alarm asserting that R6 or R7 "crashed and is down" and is blackholing
backbone traffic does not reflect reality. Direct inspection of both devices
shows no fault whatsoever.

R6 (router-id 16.1.4.1) is fully operational: all four physical interfaces
(eth0–eth3) are in UP/UP state, the FRR daemon is active, and it maintains
three simultaneous OSPF Full adjacencies — one to R4 (via eth0, segment S),
one to R5 (via eth1, segment V), and one to R7 (via eth2, segment Y). Its
routing table is complete, covering all prefixes in the topology via correct
next-hops. R7 (router-id 67.1.1.2) is equally healthy: both interfaces are
up, FRR is running, and it holds a Full OSPF adjacency to R6. All routes on
R7 are learned correctly via R6, which is expected for a single-homed stub
router.

End-to-end reachability through the alleged failed path was confirmed
experimentally: pings from R3, R9, and R1 all reach R7 with 0% packet loss,
and pc0 reaches pc12 across the full topology (0% loss, TTL=61). No
blackholing, no routing gaps, and no OSPF instability were detected anywhere
in the network.

Root cause: There is no fault. The transient NOC alert was a false alarm; R6
and R7 are operating normally and no corrective action is required.
=== END ===
